Secondary-structure composition across steering weights, pooled across all five targets (150 designs per weight, with equal contributions from each target). PyDSSP assigns secondary structure from final binder structures predicted by the primary critic and experimental structures for the natural reference.
:::


@fig-scan-1 shows that penalizing helix-like distances increases both sheet and loop content. At the strongest weights, helices are almost absent, while sheets account for just over half of the mean composition.

The pooled distributions in @fig-scan-2 reveal heterogeneous responses to intermediate steering weights. In particular, at $w=0.5$, the helix-fraction distribution appears bimodal, with some designs remaining strongly helical, while others contain little helix. This pattern suggests that modest steering can substantially redirect some optimization trajectories while leaving others helix-rich. Both helix-rich and helix-poor designs also occur within individual targets (@fig-generality).

### The response occurs on all five targets

We next examined how individual targets contribute to the pooled response. @fig-generality shows the distributions of helix, sheet, and loop fractions for every target and weight.

::: {#cell-fig-generality .cell execution_count=8}
``` {.python .cell-code code-fold="true" code-summary="Compare compositions by target"}
TARGET_ORDER = (
    steered[steered["arm"] == "helix_mean_w05"]
    .groupby("target")["helix (fraction 0-1)"]
    .median()
    .sort_values()
    .index.tolist()
)
figures.scan_boxplots(steered, TARGET_ORDER, ARMS, ("helix", "sheet", "loop"))
plt.show()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Final secondary-structure fractions across all five targets and steering weights (30 designs per target and weight). Rows show helix, sheet, and loop fractions; columns show targets. Points show individual designs.](index_files/figure-html/fig-generality-output-1.png){#fig-generality width=1117 height=936 fig-alt='Boxplots of helix, sheet, and loop fractions across steering weights for five targets.'}
:::
:::


Median helix decreased with weight on all five targets. Some targets, such as CD45, responded more strongly to intermediate weights than others, such as PD-L1, yet all reached a median helix fraction of zero by weight 5.0. Sheet and loop proportions increased as helix decreased, yielding sheet-rich designs with substantial loop content. The broad distributions at intermediate weights show that the same steering weight can yield very different compositions across seeds, as observed in @fig-scan-2.

### Steering lowers confidence

Steering produces more sheet-rich designs, but does it retain enough high-confidence candidates to make that change useful for a design campaign? We assessed confidence in the binder–target arrangement using the interface predicted template modeling score (ipTM), and confidence in local binder structure using the binder's predicted local distance difference test (pLDDT) score. @fig-cost and @tbl-cost show how confidence changes across the full population of designs.

::: {#cell-fig-cost .cell execution_count=9}
``` {.python .cell-code code-fold="true" code-summary="Compare final confidence distributions by target and steering weight"}
figures.scan_boxplots(steered, TARGET_ORDER, ARMS, ("iptm", "plddt"))
plt.show()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![ipTM (top) and pLDDT (bottom), both from the primary critic, across steering weights, with 30 designs per target and weight. Points show individual designs.](index_files/figure-html/fig-cost-output-1.png){#fig-cost width=1117 height=680 fig-alt='Boxplots of interface and binder confidence across steering weights for five targets.'}
:::
:::


::: {#tbl-cost .cell tbl-cap='Median primary-critic ipTM and binder pLDDT by target and steering weight.' execution_count=10}
``` {.python .cell-code code-fold="true" code-summary="Calculate median confidence by target and steering weight"}
from analysis import tables
from IPython.display import display

cost_metric_labels = {
    "iptm (0-1)": "ipTM",
    "binder pLDDT (0-100)": "Binder pLDDT",
}
cost_tables = {}
for column, label in cost_metric_labels.items():
    cost_tables[label] = (
        steered.groupby(["target", "arm"])[column]
        .median()
        .unstack("arm")
        .reindex(index=TARGET_ORDER, columns=ARMS)
    )

cost_table = pd.concat(cost_tables, names=["Metric", "Target"])
display(tables.confidence(cost_table))
```

